//! Caravan untyped retyping -- the memory a dependent receives, carved from a
//! region with a real floor, and the one kernel refusal that only a floor can
//! answer.
//!
//! WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS. `refusals.rye` read seL4's own compiled enums and
//! found eleven named answers a capability invocation may receive, of which
//! Caravan's policy table answers four. It named, for each of the seven
//! remaining, the subsystem that owes it -- and **untyped retyping** owes
//! `seL4_NotEnoughMemory`, with its reason written out in advance: *memory a
//! dependent receives is carved from an untyped region with a real floor*.
//! Neither the policy table nor the derivation tree can answer it. A table says
//! who may ask; a tree says whose reach a grant descends from. Only a region
//! that knows how much of itself is spent can say there is too little left.
//!
//! THE REFUSAL, AS THIS MODULE MEANS IT.
//!
//!   not_enough_memory  The region holds too little to carve what was asked.
//!                      The floor is measured after alignment, never before,
//!                      which is the whole reason this rung is a module rather
//!                      than a subtraction.
//!
//! WHAT ALIGNMENT ACTUALLY COSTS, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. seL4 places
//! every object at an address that is a multiple of the object's own size, so
//! the free watermark climbs to the next aligned boundary before a carve
//! begins, and **the padding is spent memory**. Sixteen bytes of endpoint
//! followed by a page costs four thousand and ninety-six bytes of climb, and a
//! region yields correspondingly fewer objects than a division would predict.
//! That much is plain.
//!
//! The tempting next sentence -- *so a raw remainder over-promises, and a
//! region with a page of bytes free can be unable to hold a page* -- is
//! **false here**, and this module states the reason rather than shipping the
//! flourish. Because an seL4 untyped region is naturally aligned and its width
//! is a power of two, any carve big enough to be worth asking about forces the
//! region to be at least as wide as the object, which makes the region's origin
//! aligned to the object too, which makes the bytes from an aligned start to
//! the region's end an exact multiple of the object's size. A requested size is
//! also such a multiple, and the padding is always strictly smaller than one
//! object -- so a request can never land in the gap between the two readings.
//! **The aligned floor and the naive remainder agree, always.**
//!
//! That agreement is a *consequence* of natural alignment rather than a licence
//! to drop it. `open` refusing a misaligned origin is what buys it, and the
//! self-test proves the equivalence by sweeping every kind, every count, and
//! every reachable watermark rather than by asserting it once. The day a region
//! is allowed to begin off its own boundary, the two readings part and this
//! module reds.
//!
//! THE FOUR PROPERTIES IT KEEPS.
//!
//!   Every carved object begins on a boundary of its own size. seL4's rule,
//!   held as a postcondition rather than as a habit.
//!
//!   The padding that puts it there is spent against the region, so a carve
//!   that reports room genuinely has it and the region's yield is honest.
//!
//!   A refused carve spends nothing. The watermark never moves on a refusal,
//!   so a region cannot be eaten by requests it declined.
//!
//!   A naturally aligned region needs no second opinion. The aligned floor and
//!   the plain remainder agree across the whole reachable space, proven by
//!   sweep, and the refusal at `open` is the reason.
//!
//! WHAT IS BOUNDED. A region's `size_bits` stands between seL4's own
//! `seL4_MinUntypedBits` and `seL4_MaxUntypedBits`, and one carve asks for at
//! most `max_objects` objects. Both bounds refuse with **named Rye errors**
//! rather than with a kernel refusal, since they are this module's own capacity
//! and this rung does not claim the general argument-checking debt that
//! `refusals.rye` still holds open.
//!
//! HONEST NOTE ON THE OBJECT SIZES. seL4 states some object sizes
//! unconditionally and others behind kernel configuration: in the very header
//! these are read from, `seL4_TCBBits` is 11 with an FPU and 10 without, and
//! `seL4_NotificationBits` is 6 under MCS and 5 without. A userland that
//! recited one number for either would be right on one build and wrong on
//! another. So the kinds carved here are exactly the ones seL4 states with no
//! `#ifdef` above them -- a CNode slot, an endpoint, and a page -- and the
//! configured sizes are left to the day a real kernel configuration is read
//! rather than assumed.
//!
//! CLEAN ROOM. No seL4 line is reproduced. Every size is taken from the
//! vendored BSD-2-Clause `sel4_arch/constants.h`, and the witness re-reads that
//! header at run time, so a vendored bump that moves one reds this rung on the
//! lap it lands.
//!
//! Usage:
//!   untyped          Run the self-test (default)
//!   untyped selftest Same as default
//!   untyped table    Print the kinds, their sizes, and a worked carve

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const refusals = @import("refusals.rye");

const caravan_untyped_version = "20260821.060758";

/// The smallest untyped region seL4 will name, its `seL4_MinUntypedBits`. Read
/// from the vendored `sel4_arch/constants.h` on `20260821.060758`; the witness
/// re-reads it on every run.
pub const min_untyped_bits: u32 = 4;

/// The largest, seL4's `seL4_MaxUntypedBits`, from the same header.
pub const max_untyped_bits: u32 = 38;

/// How many objects one carve may ask for. This is Caravan's own bound rather
/// than seL4's -- a supervisor hands a dependent a cohort of objects, never an
/// unbounded fleet, and a request past this is answered before any arithmetic.
pub const max_objects: u32 = 64;

/// What this module refuses with when its own bounds are reached. These are Rye
/// errors rather than kernel refusals on purpose -- the kernel offers no answer
/// for "the userland asked for a region size that is not a region size."
pub const UntypedError = error{
    RegionTooSmall,
    RegionTooLarge,
    RegionMisaligned,
    TooManyObjects,
    EmptyRequest,
};

// =========================================================================
// The object kinds, each with the size seL4 states for it
// =========================================================================

/// The kinds Caravan carves. Each is one seL4 states **unconditionally** on
/// riscv64; the configured sizes are deliberately absent, for the reason the
/// header note above gives.
pub const ObjectKind = enum(u8) {
    slot = 0,
    endpoint = 1,
    page = 2,
};

/// The whole roster, in declaration order. One list, so no count is recited.
pub const all_object_kinds = [_]ObjectKind{ .slot, .endpoint, .page };

/// How many bits wide one object of this kind is -- so its size is two raised
/// to this, and its alignment is the same number.
///
/// `seL4_SlotBits` is 5, `seL4_EndpointBits` is 4, and `seL4_PageBits` is 12,
/// each stated with no `#ifdef` above it in the vendored riscv64 header.
pub fn object_bits(kind: ObjectKind) u32 {
    // invariant: the switch is total over the roster.
    const bits: u32 = switch (kind) {
        .slot => 5,
        .endpoint => 4,
        .page => 12,
    };
    // invariant: an object smaller than the smallest untyped could never be carved.
    assert(bits >= min_untyped_bits);
    // invariant: an object larger than the largest untyped could never be seated.
    assert(bits <= max_untyped_bits);
    return bits;
}

/// How many bytes one object of this kind occupies.
pub fn object_bytes(kind: ObjectKind) u64 {
    const bits = object_bits(kind);
    const bytes: u64 = @as(u64, 1) << @intCast(bits);
    // invariant: a size in bytes is always at least one.
    assert(bytes >= 1);
    return bytes;
}

/// The name each kind carries in Caravan's own vocabulary, so a printed table
/// reads as supervision rather than as numbers.
pub fn kind_name(kind: ObjectKind) []const u8 {
    const name: []const u8 = switch (kind) {
        .slot => "slot",
        .endpoint => "endpoint",
        .page => "page",
    };
    assert(name.len > 0); // invariant: a named kind always carries its name
    return name;
}

// =========================================================================
// Alignment -- the arithmetic the floor rests on
// =========================================================================

/// The next address at or above `value` that is a multiple of two raised to
/// `bits`. This is the whole reason a remainder is a lie: the bytes between
/// `value` and its answer are spent, and no object of this size can reach them.
pub fn align_up(value: u64, bits: u32) u64 {
    // invariant: an alignment past the largest untyped could never be met.
    assert(bits <= max_untyped_bits);
    const step: u64 = @as(u64, 1) << @intCast(bits);
    const mask: u64 = step - 1;
    const aligned: u64 = (value + mask) & ~mask;
    // invariant: aligning never moves an address backward.
    assert(aligned >= value);
    // invariant: the answer stands on a boundary of the requested size.
    assert(aligned % step == 0);
    return aligned;
}

/// Whether `value` already stands on a boundary of two raised to `bits`.
pub fn aligned_to(value: u64, bits: u32) bool {
    assert(bits <= max_untyped_bits); // invariant: the alignment is one this module can name
    const step: u64 = @as(u64, 1) << @intCast(bits);
    return value % step == 0;
}

// =========================================================================
// The region
// =========================================================================

/// One untyped region: where it begins, how wide it is, and how much of it has
/// been spent.
///
/// Invariants held by the methods rather than by the struct: `origin` stands on
/// a boundary of the region's own size, as every seL4 untyped does, and
/// `watermark` never passes `bytes()`.
pub const Region = struct {
    origin: u64 = 0,
    size_bits: u32 = min_untyped_bits,
    watermark: u64 = 0,

    /// Open a region at `origin`, two raised to `size_bits` bytes wide, with
    /// nothing yet spent.
    ///
    /// Refuses by named Rye error a size outside seL4's own untyped limits, and
    /// an origin that does not stand on a boundary of the region's own size --
    /// an untyped region in seL4 is naturally aligned, and a region that is not
    /// would hand out objects whose addresses no kernel would honor.
    pub fn open(origin: u64, size_bits: u32) UntypedError!Region {
        if (size_bits < min_untyped_bits) return UntypedError.RegionTooSmall;
        if (size_bits > max_untyped_bits) return UntypedError.RegionTooLarge;
        if (!aligned_to(origin, size_bits)) return UntypedError.RegionMisaligned;

        const region: Region = .{ .origin = origin, .size_bits = size_bits, .watermark = 0 };
        // invariant: a fresh region has spent nothing.
        assert(region.watermark == 0);
        // invariant: a fresh region is a whole one.
        assert(region.whole());
        return region;
    }

    /// How many bytes the region holds in all.
    pub fn bytes(self: *const Region) u64 {
        assert(self.size_bits <= max_untyped_bits); // invariant: the width is one this module can name
        const total: u64 = @as(u64, 1) << @intCast(self.size_bits);
        assert(total >= 1); // invariant: a region always holds at least one byte
        return total;
    }

    /// How many bytes stand unspent. This number is **honest yet insufficient**
    /// on its own: alignment may make some of it unreachable for a given kind,
    /// which is exactly what `room_for` measures and a bare remainder does not.
    pub fn free_bytes(self: *const Region) u64 {
        const total = self.bytes();
        assert(self.watermark <= total); // invariant: nothing was spent that was never there
        return total - self.watermark;
    }

    /// Where a carve of this kind would begin, as an offset into the region --
    /// the watermark climbed to the next boundary of the object's own size.
    pub fn next_start(self: *const Region, kind: ObjectKind) u64 {
        const bits = object_bits(kind);
        const address = align_up(self.origin + self.watermark, bits);
        // invariant: the answer stands on a boundary of the object's own size.
        assert(aligned_to(address, bits));
        // invariant: aligning never reaches back past what is already spent.
        assert(address >= self.origin + self.watermark);
        return address - self.origin;
    }

    /// Whether `count` objects of this kind honestly fit, padding counted.
    pub fn room_for(self: *const Region, kind: ObjectKind, count: u32) bool {
        const start = self.next_start(kind);
        const each = object_bytes(kind);
        const needed = each * @as(u64, count);
        const total = self.bytes();
        if (start > total) return false;
        // invariant: the remaining span is real once the start stands inside the region.
        assert(start <= total);
        return needed <= total - start;
    }

    /// Carve `count` objects of `kind` from the region.
    ///
    /// Answers `.no_error` when the objects are seated, and
    /// `.not_enough_memory` when the region holds too little **after
    /// alignment** -- the refusal this module owes. A refusal spends nothing:
    /// the watermark stands exactly where it stood.
    ///
    /// Refuses by named Rye error a request for no objects at all, or for more
    /// than one carve may ask.
    pub fn retype(self: *Region, kind: ObjectKind, count: u32) UntypedError!refusals.KernelRefusal {
        if (count == 0) return UntypedError.EmptyRequest;
        if (count > max_objects) return UntypedError.TooManyObjects;

        const before = self.watermark;

        if (!self.room_for(kind, count)) {
            // invariant: a refused carve leaves the region exactly as it found it.
            assert(self.watermark == before);
            assert(self.whole());
            return .not_enough_memory;
        }

        const start = self.next_start(kind);
        const each = object_bytes(kind);
        self.watermark = start + each * @as(u64, count);

        // invariant: the first object stands on a boundary of its own size.
        assert(aligned_to(self.origin + start, object_bits(kind)));
        // invariant: a seated carve always spends at least what it asked for.
        assert(self.watermark >= before + each * @as(u64, count));
        // invariant: the region never spends more than it holds.
        assert(self.whole());
        return .no_error;
    }

    /// Where the `index`-th object of the most recent carve of `kind` would
    /// stand, given a carve that began at `start`. Every one lands on a
    /// boundary of its own size, since the first does and each is exactly one
    /// object wide.
    pub fn object_address(self: *const Region, kind: ObjectKind, start: u64, index: u32) u64 {
        assert(index < max_objects); // invariant: the index stands inside one carve's bound
        const each = object_bytes(kind);
        const address = self.origin + start + each * @as(u64, index);
        // invariant: every object of a carve stands on a boundary of its own size.
        assert(aligned_to(address, object_bits(kind)));
        return address;
    }

    /// Re-prove the whole region: a width seL4 would name, an origin standing on
    /// its own boundary, and a watermark inside what the region holds. Returns
    /// true when all three hold.
    pub fn whole(self: *const Region) bool {
        if (self.size_bits < min_untyped_bits) return false;
        if (self.size_bits > max_untyped_bits) return false;
        if (!aligned_to(self.origin, self.size_bits)) return false;
        const total: u64 = @as(u64, 1) << @intCast(self.size_bits);
        if (self.watermark > total) return false;
        return true;
    }
};

// =========================================================================
// The kinds and a worked carve, printed
// =========================================================================

fn print_table() u8 {
    // A region one page wide, opened at a page boundary. The carve below spends
    // a single endpoint first, which leaves the watermark sixteen bytes in --
    // far short of a page boundary, so the page that follows it is refused even
    // though thousands of bytes stand unspent. That is the whole lesson in one
    // printed pair of lines.
    var region = Region.open(0x8000_0000, 12) catch return 1;

    print("untyped: version {s}\n", .{caravan_untyped_version});
    for (all_object_kinds) |kind| {
        print("untyped: kind {s} is {d} bits, {d} bytes, aligned to its own size\n", .{
            kind_name(kind),
            object_bits(kind),
            object_bytes(kind),
        });
    }

    const first = region.retype(.endpoint, 1) catch return 1;
    print("untyped: carving 1 endpoint answers {s} -- {d} bytes spent, {d} free\n", .{
        refusals.kernel_symbol(first),
        region.watermark,
        region.free_bytes(),
    });

    const second = region.retype(.page, 1) catch return 1;
    print("untyped: carving 1 page answers {s} -- {d} bytes free, yet the next page boundary stands at {d}\n", .{
        refusals.kernel_symbol(second),
        region.free_bytes(),
        region.next_start(.page),
    });
    print("untyped: {s} -- {s}\n", .{
        refusals.kernel_symbol(.not_enough_memory),
        refusals.kernel_meaning(.not_enough_memory),
    });
    return 0;
}

// =========================================================================
// Self-test -- the asserts are the specification
// =========================================================================

/// Movement one -- a region opens with a width seL4 would name, and refuses by
/// name every width and origin it would not.
fn prove_opening() void {
    var region = try_region(Region.open(0x8000_0000, 20));
    assert(region.size_bits == 20);
    assert(region.watermark == 0);
    assert(region.bytes() == @as(u64, 1) << 20);
    assert(region.free_bytes() == region.bytes());
    assert(region.whole());

    // A width below seL4's own smallest untyped is refused by name.
    if (Region.open(0, min_untyped_bits - 1)) |_| {
        assert(false); // invariant: a region narrower than the kernel's smallest never opens
    } else |err| {
        assert(err == UntypedError.RegionTooSmall);
    }

    // A width past its largest, likewise.
    if (Region.open(0, max_untyped_bits + 1)) |_| {
        assert(false); // invariant: a region wider than the kernel's largest never opens
    } else |err| {
        assert(err == UntypedError.RegionTooLarge);
    }

    // An origin that does not stand on a boundary of the region's own size is
    // refused: every untyped in seL4 is naturally aligned.
    if (Region.open(0x8000_0008, 12)) |_| {
        assert(false); // invariant: a misaligned region never opens
    } else |err| {
        assert(err == UntypedError.RegionMisaligned);
    }

    // Both ends of the honest range open.
    assert(try_region(Region.open(0, min_untyped_bits)).size_bits == min_untyped_bits);
    assert(try_region(Region.open(0, max_untyped_bits)).size_bits == max_untyped_bits);
}

/// Movement two -- the refusal this module owes, measured after alignment, and
/// a floor that refuses one request without closing the region.
fn prove_alignment_floor() void {
    var region = try_region(Region.open(0x8000_0000, 12));

    // One endpoint, sixteen bytes wide, spends sixteen bytes.
    assert(try_answer(region.retype(.endpoint, 1)) == .no_error);
    assert(region.watermark == 16);

    // Four thousand and eighty bytes stand unspent -- a whole page short by
    // sixteen. A page needs four thousand and ninety-six of them *and* a page
    // boundary to begin on, and the next such boundary is the far end of the
    // region. So the honest answer is the refusal this module owes.
    assert(region.free_bytes() == 4080);
    assert(region.next_start(.page) == 4096);
    assert(!region.room_for(.page, 1));
    assert(try_answer(region.retype(.page, 1)) == .not_enough_memory);

    // And the region is untouched by the refusal.
    assert(region.watermark == 16);
    assert(region.free_bytes() == 4080);
    assert(region.whole());

    // Slots, being far narrower, still fit in what remains -- the region is not
    // exhausted, it is merely unable to seat a page. A floor refuses one
    // request without closing the region.
    assert(try_answer(region.retype(.slot, 8)) == .no_error);
    assert(region.watermark == 32 + 8 * 32);
    assert(region.whole());
}

/// Movement three -- every carved object stands on a boundary of its own size,
/// and the padding that puts it there is counted against the region.
fn prove_alignment_holds() void {
    var region = try_region(Region.open(0x4000_0000, 16));

    // A single slot leaves the watermark thirty-two bytes in, which is not a
    // page boundary. The page that follows must climb to one, and the climb is
    // spent memory.
    assert(try_answer(region.retype(.slot, 1)) == .no_error);
    assert(region.watermark == 32);

    const page_start = region.next_start(.page);
    assert(page_start == 4096);
    assert(try_answer(region.retype(.page, 1)) == .no_error);
    assert(region.watermark == 4096 + 4096);

    // The padding was really spent: the region has given out one slot and one
    // page, thirty-two plus four thousand and ninety-six bytes of object, and
    // yet eight thousand one hundred and ninety-two bytes stand behind the
    // watermark. The difference is the climb, and counting it is the point.
    assert(region.watermark - (32 + 4096) == 4064);

    // Every object of a carve lands on its own boundary, first to last.
    var index: u32 = 0;
    while (index < 4) : (index += 1) {
        const address = region.object_address(.page, page_start, index);
        assert(aligned_to(address, object_bits(.page)));
    }
    assert(region.whole());
}

/// Movement four -- a region spends until it honestly cannot, and every refusal
/// after that leaves it exactly where it stood.
fn prove_exhaustion() void {
    var region = try_region(Region.open(0x1_0000, 12));

    // A page-wide region seats exactly one page and no more.
    assert(try_answer(region.retype(.page, 1)) == .no_error);
    assert(region.free_bytes() == 0);
    assert(try_answer(region.retype(.page, 1)) == .not_enough_memory);
    assert(try_answer(region.retype(.slot, 1)) == .not_enough_memory);
    assert(try_answer(region.retype(.endpoint, 1)) == .not_enough_memory);
    assert(region.watermark == 4096);
    assert(region.whole());

    // A carve larger than the whole region is refused rather than partially
    // seated -- a floor answers the request that was made, never a smaller one.
    var fresh = try_region(Region.open(0x1_0000, 12));
    assert(try_answer(fresh.retype(.page, 2)) == .not_enough_memory);
    assert(fresh.watermark == 0);
    assert(fresh.free_bytes() == fresh.bytes());

    // The same region seats the whole of what it can hold in slots, in one
    // carve, and refuses one slot past it.
    assert(try_answer(fresh.retype(.slot, 64)) == .no_error);
    assert(fresh.watermark == 64 * 32);
    assert(fresh.free_bytes() == 4096 - 2048);
    assert(fresh.whole());
}

/// Movement five -- the claim this module refused to overstate, proven by
/// sweep rather than asserted once.
///
/// A naturally aligned, power-of-two region needs no second opinion: reading
/// the floor after alignment and reading it as a plain remainder give the same
/// answer for every kind, every count, and every watermark a region can reach.
/// The reason is arithmetic, and it is worth holding in view -- any carve large
/// enough to matter forces the region to be at least as wide as the object,
/// which makes the origin aligned to the object, which makes the span from an
/// aligned start to the region's end an exact multiple of the object's size.
/// The request is such a multiple too, and the padding is always strictly less
/// than one object, so no request can land between the two readings.
///
/// What this movement really guards is `open`'s refusal of a misaligned origin.
/// That refusal is what buys the agreement; drop it, and the two readings part.
fn prove_readings_agree() void {
    var kinds_swept: u32 = 0;
    for (all_object_kinds) |kind| {
        const each = object_bytes(kind);

        var size_bits: u32 = min_untyped_bits;
        while (size_bits <= 20) : (size_bits += 1) {
            // A handful of origins, each naturally aligned as `open` requires,
            // so the sweep covers regions placed high as well as at zero.
            const step: u64 = @as(u64, 1) << @intCast(size_bits);
            var which: u32 = 0;
            while (which < 3) : (which += 1) {
                const origin = step * @as(u64, which) * 7;
                var region = try_region(Region.open(origin, size_bits));
                const total = region.bytes();

                // Walk the watermark across the whole region in strides fine
                // enough to land off every object boundary in play.
                var watermark: u64 = 0;
                while (watermark <= total) : (watermark += 8) {
                    region.watermark = watermark;

                    var count: u32 = 1;
                    while (count <= max_objects) : (count += 1) {
                        const needed = each * @as(u64, count);
                        const plain = needed <= total - watermark;
                        // invariant: the aligned reading and the plain remainder
                        // agree, because the region is naturally aligned.
                        assert(region.room_for(kind, count) == plain);
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        kinds_swept += 1;
    }
    // invariant: the sweep covered every kind on the roster.
    assert(kinds_swept == all_object_kinds.len);
}

/// Movement six -- the module's own bounds, which refuse by named Rye error
/// rather than by a kernel answer, because the kernel offers no word for "the
/// userland asked for nothing" or "the userland asked for more than one carve
/// may hold."
fn prove_bounds() void {
    var region = try_region(Region.open(0, max_untyped_bits));

    // A carve of no objects is refused by name, and seats nothing.
    if (region.retype(.slot, 0)) |_| {
        assert(false); // invariant: a carve of nothing never seats
    } else |err| {
        assert(err == UntypedError.EmptyRequest);
    }
    assert(region.watermark == 0);

    // A carve past this module's own bound is refused by name, even though the
    // region is vast enough to hold it -- the bound is Caravan's, not seL4's.
    if (region.retype(.slot, max_objects + 1)) |_| {
        assert(false); // invariant: a carve past the bound never seats
    } else |err| {
        assert(err == UntypedError.TooManyObjects);
    }
    assert(region.watermark == 0);

    // Exactly the bound is welcome.
    assert(try_answer(region.retype(.slot, max_objects)) == .no_error);
    assert(region.watermark == max_objects * 32);
    assert(region.whole());
}

fn run_self_test() u8 {
    prove_opening();
    prove_alignment_floor();
    prove_alignment_holds();
    prove_exhaustion();
    prove_readings_agree();
    prove_bounds();

    // The debt is answered in the kernel's own vocabulary, taken from the module
    // that reads the eleven out of seL4's header rather than from memory.
    assert(refusals.owed_by(.not_enough_memory) == .untyped_retyping);

    print("untyped: version {s}\n", .{caravan_untyped_version});
    print("untyped: memory a dependent receives is carved from a region with a real floor.\n", .{});
    print("untyped: a carve the region cannot seat after alignment answers {s}.\n", .{
        refusals.kernel_symbol(.not_enough_memory),
    });
    print("untyped: every object stands on a boundary of its own size, and the padding that puts it there is spent.\n", .{});
    print("untyped: a refused carve spends nothing -- the watermark stands exactly where it stood.\n", .{});
    print("untyped: a naturally aligned region needs no second opinion -- the aligned floor and the plain remainder agree across every kind, count, and watermark swept.\n", .{});
    print("untyped: a region stands between {d} and {d} bits and carves at most {d} objects at once, both bounds refusing by named error.\n", .{ min_untyped_bits, max_untyped_bits, max_objects });
    print("untyped: floor measured after alignment, refusal spending nothing, the owed refusal answered -- GREEN.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

/// Unwrap an answer the self-test expects to succeed, so a bound refusing where
/// it should not aborts loudly rather than reading as a mismatch.
fn try_answer(result: UntypedError!refusals.KernelRefusal) refusals.KernelRefusal {
    const answer = result catch {
        assert(false); // invariant: this call is inside every bound the module names
        unreachable;
    };
    // invariant: every answer is one of the kernel's eleven.
    assert(@intFromEnum(answer) < refusals.kernel_refusal_count);
    return answer;
}

/// Unwrap a region the self-test expects to open, for the same reason.
fn try_region(result: UntypedError!Region) Region {
    const region = result catch {
        assert(false); // invariant: this region stands inside every bound the module names
        unreachable;
    };
    assert(region.whole()); // invariant: an opened region is a whole one
    return region;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    const allocator = init.arena.allocator();
    const args = try init.minimal.args.toSlice(allocator);

    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "table")) return print_table();
    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "selftest")) return run_self_test();
    if (args.len >= 2) return 1;
    return run_self_test();
}
